UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Cultures in our community', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable sources, not from assumptions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort into language, food, custom or celebration: Auslan; dumplings; shaking hands when meeting; Lunar New Year.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAuslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a culture web for yourself or for a published example using five categories. Add one thing that is shared with others in your community and one that is different. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable sources, not from assumptions.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a culture web for yourself or for a published example using five categories. Add one thing that is shared with others in your community and one that is different. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. Reasoning: I used culture by attending to language and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Correction: Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. Why: the correction restores accurate use of culture.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating culture on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with culture, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. This matches the lesson skill for culture.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration.","Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Sort into language, food, custom or celebration: Auslan; dumplings; shaking hands when meeting; Lunar New Year. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures,…","Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration.","Careful use of language and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named language and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the language and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising culture. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about culture."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about language and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists language, favourite…","Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences.","Make a culture web for yourself or for a published example using five categories. Add one thing that is shared with others in your community and one…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating culture on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. Reasoning: I used culture by attending to language and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with culture, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Correction: Auslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration. Why: the correction restores accurate use of culture.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w31-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise culture.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use culture with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify language and explain why it matters.","I can practise custom independently on a new prompt.","I can notice diversity needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable…","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable sources, not from assumptions.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists language, favourite foods, a festival and a family custom, with notes on which parts the family chose to share.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort into language, food, custom or celebration: Auslan; dumplings; shaking hands when meeting; Lunar New Year.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAuslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration.","explain":"Culture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable sources, not from assumptions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Culture and diversity in one school.","caption":"Culture includes more than food.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Culture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">language, food, customs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Families</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">12 languages at home</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists langu Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists langu Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists language, favourite foods, a festival and a family custom, with notes on which parts the family chose to share. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A personal culture web.","caption":"Choose what to share.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Web</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">my language, my festival</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Share</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">only what I choose</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Here is a more productive model: Example: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists language, favourite foods, a festival and a family custom, with notes on which parts the family chose to share.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAuslan: language; dumplings: food; shaking hands: custom; Lunar New Year: celebration.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing everyone from a country shares exactly the same culture ignores individual and family differences. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about culture?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCulture is the way a group of people live, including language, food, beliefs, customs, arts, clothing and celebrations. A community can include many cultures, and each person can belong to more than one. We learn about cultures from the people themselves and from reliable sources, not from assumptions. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 1: Cultures in our community","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Cultures and communities","week":31,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a school finds that families speak twelve different languages at home. A culture web for one family lists language, favourite foods, a festival and a family custom, with notes on which parts the family chose to share.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Culture and diversity in one school.","caption":"Culture includes more than food.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Culture</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">language, food, customs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Families</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">12 languages at home</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A personal culture web.","caption":"Choose what to share.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Web</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">my language, my festival</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Share</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">only what I choose</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w31-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
